:Info: explore: 2026-09-07  explore

Syntax:  explore

Function: Explore is a cave-exploration adventure game.  You wander a
cave of 58 rooms, collect treasures, defeat or evade its creatures,
learn its magic words, and carry your finds back to the surface.  A
perfect score is 500 points and earns the rank of Grand Master
Explorer.

Explore was written in 1980 by Jim Lippard and reconstructed for the
Multics simulator in 2026.

Playing: Move with one-word compass directions (n, s, e, w, u, d) and
in/out; you may also type the name of a nearby room to walk there.
Common commands: get, drop, throw, look, read, what (inventory), score,
save, restore, brief, full, help <topic>.  Type "commands" in the game
for the full list and "help" for information.

Score by carrying treasures to the shack and dropping them there;
defeating creatures and solving certain puzzles also scores.

See also the topics below (help explore.<topic>):
   setup        installing and setting up the game
   playing      commands and how to play
   scoring      how points and ranks work
   multiplayer  playing with others; the multip attribute

:Info: explore.setup: 2026-09-07  explore setup

The bound object bound_explore_ is installed in >aml by the library
maintainer.  Everything else is set up with explore_setup.ec, which
creates >site>explore_dir and populates it:

   asp ec >aml
   ec explore_setup

This creates >site>explore_dir (read-only base data), a writable
directory (default >site>explore_dir>private), and
>site>explore_dir>explore.rwdir.

explore.rwdir controls the installation mode, read at startup:
   line 1  the read-write directory pathname, or "none" for read-only
   line 2  (optional) "multip" to permit multiplayer; "^multip" or an
           absent second line means writable single-player only

So: no rwdir or line 1 = "none" runs read-only (no saves to shared
files, no winners recorded, no multiplayer); a path with no multip line
runs writable single-player; a path with a "multip" second line permits
multiplayer.  Even then, a player may only enable per-user multip if
the administrator has granted them write access to the shared files; a
player without it stays single-player.

Before real play, edit hours.data so line 1 is the rot13 of your chosen
sorcerer word.  See the README for ACL grants and details.

:Info: explore.playing: 2026-09-07  explore playing

Movement: n s e w u d (north south east west up down), and in / out.
You may also type the name of a nearby room to move there.

Objects:  get <thing>, drop <thing>, get all, drop all, throw <thing>,
eat <thing>, read <thing>, wave / swing, plug, what (lists what you
carry).

Information: look (reprint the room), commands, help <topic>, info,
news, score, turns, modes.

Game: save [name], restore [name], quit (also stop / end).  The
brief / full / fast modes control how much room description you see
(full = always long, the default; brief = long on first visit then
short; fast = always short).  The quit / ^quit modes control the break
key: "quit" (the default) leaves it working normally; "^quit" disables
it so you cannot accidentally break out of the game.

Certain words carved on walls or spoken by cave dwellers are magic;
learn and use them.  Keep an eye out for anything unusual -- some rooms
and objects reward a little curiosity.

:Info: explore.scoring: 2026-09-07  explore scoring

The maximum score is 500 points.  You earn points chiefly by carrying
treasures back to the shack and dropping them there, and by defeating
the cave's creatures.  A few other accomplishments also count --
discovering them is part of the game.

Your rank rises with your score, up to Grand Master Explorer at a
perfect 500.  Type "score" in the game to see how you are doing.

:Info: explore.multiplayer: 2026-09-07  explore multiplayer

Some installations allow several people to share one cave.  Where it is
permitted, and where the administrator has given you access, you can
join the shared game.

Joining is opt-in via the "multip" mode, which is OFF by default.  Turn
it on with the "multip" command, or by default by putting the line

   multip

in your start_up.explore (read from your home directory at launch).
With multip on, you become visible to the other players and to the
sorcerer, and you can message a player in your room with "talk".  Turn
it back off with "^multip".  If multiplayer is not permitted here, or
you lack access, "multip" simply reports that it is unavailable.  The
objects and creatures remain private to your own game -- multip only
adds mutual visibility and messaging.
